.TH QYPR-LOCK 1 "2026-07-11" "qypr-lock" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qypr-lock \- lean GPU-free Wayland session lock screen for Hyprland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qypr-lock
[options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qypr-lock
is a memory-efficient, GPU-free session lock screen for Wayland compositors
implementing the
.B ext-session-lock-v1
protocol (such as Hyprland). It is software-rendered with cairo and
.BR wl_shm ";" it authenticates the user with PAM and, optionally, shows an
MPRIS now-playing panel, lock-screen notifications, and a video wallpaper.
.PP
Running the binary locks every connected output. Successful PAM
authentication (the
.B login
service) unlocks the session and the process exits.
.PP
When idle, only a clock and a standby power button are shown. Any key press
or pointer movement reveals the password field, the power menu, and the audio
panel. After a configurable idle period the optional video wallpaper pauses and
the screen fades to black; any input reverses it. Real backlight/DPMS power-off
is left to the idle daemon (e.g. hypridle).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout " " \fIseconds\fR
Pause the video wallpaper and dim the screen to black after
.I seconds
of idle. Default is 60. A value of 0 disables the idle dim.
.TP
.BR \-\-preview " [" \fIpath.png\fR "]"
Render the lock screen to a PNG without locking the session, for visual
verification. Writes
.I path
and
.IR path -idle.png.
If
.I path
is omitted,
.B qypr-preview.png
is used. This does not lock the session.
.TP
.BR \-\-video\-test " [" \fIseconds\fR "]"
Exercise the video pipeline offscreen (via libmpv) without locking. Runs for
.I seconds
(default 6) and then exits. Useful to verify the playlist and decode pipeline.
.TP
.BR \-\-fingerprint\-test " [" \fIseconds\fR "]"
Exercise fingerprint verification and its suspend/resume handling without
locking: claims the reader, logs every fprintd call, status and
.B PrepareForSleep
transition with wall-clock timestamps (to line up with
.BR journalctl (1)),
and exits after
.I seconds
of awake time (default 60) or on a match, with status 0. A match unlocks
nothing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage message and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Required by Wayland and PAM for socket and credential exchange.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Used to locate the per-user configuration, playlists, and the notification log
service.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/qypr/playlists/day.m3u
.TP
.I ~/.config/qypr/playlists/night.m3u
Time-of-day video playlists for the wallpaper. Entries are resolved relative
to the playlist file. When unavailable, a gradient background is shown instead.
.TP
.I ~/.config/qypr/sensitive_apps.conf
Per-app rules used by the notification layer.
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
Real lock-screen notifications are shown by observing
.B org.freedesktop.Notifications
traffic on the session bus through a D-Bus monitor connection, compatible with
any daemon (SwayNC, dunst, mako, ...). To also carry the pre-lock backlog of
undismissed notifications, enable the
.B qypr-record
mirror service; see
.BR qypr-record (1).
.SH FINGERPRINT
When
.BR fprintd (8)
has a reader and an enrolled finger, touching the reader unlocks as well; the
password keeps working at any time. qypr-lock talks to fprintd over D-Bus
directly (no PAM service is involved), ends the scan before the system
suspends while holding a logind delay inhibitor, and starts a new one on
resume. After five wrong fingers fingerprint is switched off until the
password unlocks; suspending or restarting the scan does not reset the count.
Set
.B fingerprint = false
in the
.B [lock]
section of
.I ~/.config/qypr/bar.conf
to turn it off. See docs/FINGERPRINT.md.
.SH SECURITY
The password is copied directly into a dedicated PAM worker thread and cleared
immediately after use; the UI only ever holds the in-progress input.
Authentication runs off the UI thread so a slow PAM stack cannot freeze the
lock surface. A fingerprint match is accepted only from the fprintd connection
that granted the reader's claim; forged D-Bus signals from other processes are
ignored. The lock, the unlock method (password or fingerprint) and a
fingerprint lockout are logged to the system journal under the authpriv
facility:
.BR "journalctl -t qypr-lock" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session was unlocked successfully (authentication succeeded).
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred before or during locking (e.g. Wayland/PAM setup failure),
or the process was terminated by a signal.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Lock the session now:
.B qypr-lock
.TP
Lock and dim after 30s idle:
.B qypr-lock \-\-idle\-timeout 30
.TP
Preview the look without locking:
.B qypr-lock \-\-preview /tmp/preview.png
.TP
Exercise the video wallpaper for 10s:
.B qypr-lock \-\-video\-test 10
.TP
Watch the fingerprint reader across a suspend:
.B qypr-lock \-\-fingerprint\-test 120
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qypr-record (1),
.BR hyprctl (1),
.BR hypridle (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR pam (8)
